# Conversational Vietnamese — the register the app actually emits.
#
# These are the shape of Gemini's Vietnamese translations of conversational
# English: asking prices, ordering, directions, booking, apologising. Every row
# carries at least one clause boundary where it is natural, because the TTFA
# arms split on the app's own rule and a set of boundary-free sentences would
# measure clause splitting doing nothing.
#
# ORTHOGRAPHY POLICY — load-bearing, enforced by the build check:
#   `text` spells every numeral, time and currency amount out in Vietnamese
#   words and contains no abbreviations. The round-trip scores an ASR transcript
#   against this string, and the shared normalizer leaves numbers as written by
#   design, so a digit here would cost word errors for a rendering that was in
#   fact correct. benchmarks/stt/README.md documents the failure with a worked
#   example: a hypothesis correctly saying "17:00" scores three word errors
#   against a reference reading "MƯỜI BẢY GIỜ". Keeping digits out keeps this
#   metric about the audio.
#
# CODE-SWITCH SUBSET — rows tagged `code-switch` carry embedded English, which
#   is both what ZeroTTS specifically claims and what real translated output
#   contains. KNOWN SCORING HAZARD: the normalizer splits `check-in` into two
#   tokens, so a transcript of `checkin` costs word errors regardless of how
#   intelligible the audio was. Over nine rows that is not a rate worth quoting,
#   so phase 4 reports CER as primary here and hand-reviews every hypothesis.
#
# PROVENANCE: authored for this benchmark, not drawn from a corpus, so `ref_text`
#   is the authored string rather than a human transcription. This is an
#   in-domain, author-chosen set and NOT an adversarial one — and its author is
#   also the person judging the outcome, which is a bias worth stating on the
#   artifact rather than only in the report.
